This Structural pattern solves one of the most annoying problems in all of software engineering: The Tree Structure.
The Problem: Treating Groups differently than Singles

Imagine you are building a File System for an operating system (like Windows or Mac).
You have two concepts: File and Folder.

    * A File has a size (e.g., 5MB).
    * A Folder contains a list of Files, and a list of other Folders. To get the size of a Folder, you have to add up
    the sizes of everything inside it.

The Beginner Way:
The Junior Developer writes code that uses if statements to constantly check what type of object they are dealing with:
Java

// Trying to calculate the total size of a drive
for (Object item : hardDrive) {
    if (item instanceof File) {
        totalSize += ((File) item).getSize();
    } else if (item instanceof Folder) {
        // Now I have to write a complex recursive loop just for folders!
        totalSize += calculateFolderSize((Folder) item);
    }
}

Why a Senior rejects this: Every time the client wants to do anything (copy, delete, check size), they have to write
massive instanceof checks. The code becomes a nightmare to maintain.

The Senior Solution: The Composite Pattern (The Tree)
The Composite Pattern says: "Treat individual objects and groups of objects in exactly the same way."

We create one unified Interface called FileSystemComponent.
Both File and Folder implement it.
When the client calls getSize() on a File, it just returns its size.
When the client calls getSize() on a Folder, the Folder automatically loops through everything inside itself and
calls getSize() on them!

The client can just blindly call getSize() on any object, without caring if it is a single file or a folder
containing 10,000 files!